module j3.game
    Window, input, draw, audio. C names stay private.
    SDL wrapper. game_headless.j3 is the test stand-in, not a second API.
    Main thread only: window, GL, and audio state is not thread-safe (`j3c doc threads`).

struct Color
    RGBA color (0–255 per channel).
    r: u8
        Red channel.
    g: u8
        Green channel.
    b: u8
        Blue channel.
    a: u8
        Alpha channel.
    fn Color.rgb(r: u8, g: u8, b: u8): Color
        Opaque color from RGB (alpha 255).
    fn Color.rgba(r: u8, g: u8, b: u8, a: u8): Color
        Color from RGBA.

fn rgb(r: u8, g: u8, b: u8): Color
    Opaque RGB color (alpha 255).
fn rgba(r: u8, g: u8, b: u8, a: u8): Color
    RGBA color.
struct Rgba
    Tightly packed RGBA8 image. `pixels.len` is `w * h * 4`, or 0 on failure.
    pixels: []u8
        Row-major pixels, 4 bytes each, top row first.
    w: i32
        Width in pixels.
    h: i32
        Height in pixels.

struct Texture
    GPU texture handle and pixel size.
    raw: ptr = 0
        SDL texture pointer (0 if empty).
    w: i32 = 0
        Width in pixels.
    h: i32 = 0
        Height in pixels.

struct Sound
    Loaded sound sample.
    raw: ptr
        Mixer chunk pointer (0 if empty).

struct AudioStream
    PCM output stream: push f32 interleaved samples from the main thread, SDL plays them.
    Its own audio device, separate from SDL_mixer's `Sound`/`Music`; both play at once.
    Playhead in frames = frames sent - `queued()`. `queued()` counts SDL's queue only, not
    the device buffer (512 frames), so the speakers trail it by up to that much.
    Under `SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy` the device drains in real time, so `queued()` still counts down.
    raw: int
        SDL audio device id (0 if empty).
    rate: int
        Frames per second.
    channels: int
        Interleaved channels per frame.
    fn AudioStream.queue(this: &AudioStream, samples: []f32)
        Append interleaved samples (frames * channels). Never blocks.
    fn AudioStream.queued(this: &AudioStream): int
        Frames queued but not yet played. Playhead = frames sent - queued().
    fn AudioStream.clear(this: &AudioStream)
        Drop everything queued right away (instant stop or seek).
    fn AudioStream.pause(this: &AudioStream, paused: bool)
        Pause or resume the device. Queued samples stay queued while paused.
    fn AudioStream.close(this: &AudioStream)
        Close the device; the stream is empty afterwards.

struct Music
    Loaded music stream.
    raw: ptr
        Mixer music pointer (0 if empty).

struct Font
    Opened TrueType font.
    raw: ptr
        TTF font pointer (0 if empty).

struct Camera
    2D camera: world origin and zoom.
    pos: Vec2
        World-space top-left of the view.
    zoom: f32
        Screen pixels per world unit.
    fn Camera.create(zoom: f32): Camera
        Camera at origin with zoom (minimum 0.01).
    fn Camera.toScreen(this: Camera, world: Vec2): Vec2
        Map a world point to screen space.
    fn Camera.toWorld(this: Camera, screen: Vec2): Vec2
        Map a screen point to world space.
    fn Camera.follow(this: &Camera, focus: Vec2, view: Vec2, map_w: f32, map_h: f32)
        Center on focus, clamped so the view stays inside the map.

fn tryLoadSoundC(path: cstr): Sound
    Try to load a sound from a C string path. Empty when the file or SDL_mixer is missing.
fn tryLoadSound(path: string): Sound
    Try to load a sound. Empty when the file or SDL_mixer is missing.
fn loadSound(path: string): Sound
    Load a sound sample; panics on failure.
fn play(sound: Sound): int
    Play a sound once on the sfx group. Returns the mixer channel, or `-1`.
fn playLoops(sound: Sound, loops: int): int
    Play a sound on the sfx group with a Mix-style loop count. Returns the channel, or `-1`.
fn playGroup(sound: Sound, group: int, loops: int): int
    Play `sound` on the sfx (`0`) or voice (`1`) group. Returns the channel, or `-1`.
fn setGroupVolume(group: int, volume: int)
    Set every channel in `group` to `volume` (0..128). `0` is sfx, `1` is voice.
fn fadeIn(sound: Sound, group: int, ms: int): int
    Fade `sound` in on `group` over `ms` milliseconds. Returns the channel, or `-1`.
fn fadeOut(channel: int, ms: int)
    Fade `channel` out over `ms` milliseconds. `-1` fades every channel.
fn setPosition(channel: int, angle: int, distance: int)
    Place `channel` in the stereo field. `angle` is degrees, `distance` is 0..255.
fn stop()
    Halt all sound channels.
fn destroySound(sound: Sound)
    Free a sound.
fn tryLoadMusicC(path: cstr): Music
    Try to load music from a C string path. Empty when the file or SDL_mixer is missing.
fn tryLoadMusic(path: string): Music
    Try to load music. Empty when the file or SDL_mixer is missing.
fn loadMusic(path: string): Music
    Load looping music; panics on failure.
fn playMusic(music: Music)
    Start music looping forever.
fn fadeInMusic(music: Music, ms: int)
    Fade music in, looping forever, over `ms` milliseconds.
fn fadeOutMusic(ms: int)
    Fade the playing music out over `ms` milliseconds.
fn stopMusic()
    Stop the music channel.
fn destroyMusic(music: Music)
    Free music.
fn setVolume(volume: int)
    Set all sound channel volumes (0..128).
fn setMusicVolume(volume: int)
    Set music volume (0..128).
fn openAudioStream(rate: int, channels: int): AudioStream
    Open the default output device for f32 interleaved samples. Empty stream (raw 0) when
    there is no audio device or SDL audio fails. Starts unpaused; works without a window.
fn clipboardTypes(): []string
    MIME types the system clipboard offers right now, in the owner's order (such as "image/png",
    "image/gif", "text/uri-list", "text/plain"). X11's UTF8_STRING, STRING and TEXT show up once
    as "text/plain". Empty when the clipboard is empty, can't be read, or headless.
    Main thread only. A view of a buffer that the next call replaces.
    X11 reads the CLIPBOARD selection on a display connection of its own (`libX11.so.6`).
    Wayland without `DISPLAY` asks `wl-paste` (the `wl-clipboard` package); empty when it is missing.
    An owner that doesn't answer within about N s gives empty, so the frame never hangs.
fn clipboardData(mime: string): []u8
    Bytes the clipboard owner gives for `mime` ("text/plain" also takes X11's UTF8_STRING and
    STRING), or empty when that type isn't offered or the read fails or times out (about N s with
    no answer). Large data (browsers send big images and GIFs with X11 INCR) is read in full.
    Main thread only. A view of a buffer that the next call replaces.
    if game.keyPressed(Key.V) && game.keyDown(Key.Ctrl) {
    let png = game.clipboardData("image/png");
    if png.len > 0 { io.writeFile("pasted.png", png); }
    }
enum DragState
    What the drag started by `dragFiles` is doing this frame.
    Idle
        No drag.
    Dragging
        The pointer carries the files; the button is still down.
    Dropped
        Released over an app that took the files. Lasts one frame.
    Cancelled
        Released where nothing takes files, or Escape was pressed. Lasts one frame.

fn dragFiles(paths: []string): bool
    Start dragging files out of the window into another app (a chat, a browser, a file manager).
    Call it while the left button is held, typically once the mouse has moved a few pixels from a
    press on an item. `game.poll()` moves the drag along; `dragState()` says how it ended.
    While it runs the window gets no mouse motion; the release reaches it as a normal
    `mouseReleased(Mouse.Left)`. Paths must be absolute. Drag data you only have in memory
    (a rendered image) by writing it to a file first.
    if game.mouseDown(Mouse.Left) && !started && moved > 6.0 {
    started = game.dragFiles(["/tmp/shot.png"]);
    }
    if game.dragState() == DragState.Dropped { printLn("sent"); }
    False when it cannot start: headless, no X11 (`libX11.so.6`), SDL on native Wayland (SDL2
    normally runs on XWayland, which works), the button is not held, a path is relative, the list
    is over 16 KB as URIs, or the last drop is still being delivered. Linux only. Main thread only.
fn dragState(): DragState
    The drag started by `dragFiles`: `Dragging` while it runs, then `Dropped` or `Cancelled` for one frame.
fn setColor(c: Color)
    Set the current draw color.
fn clear()
    Clear the backbuffer to black.
fn clearTo(c: Color)
    Clear the backbuffer to `c`.
fn drawRect(pos: Vec2, size: Vec2)
    Fill a screen-space axis-aligned rect.
fn drawRectCam(cam: Camera, pos: Vec2, size: Vec2)
    Fill a world-space rect through `cam`.
fn loadRgba(path: string, arena: &Arena): Rgba
    Load a PNG (or other SDL_image file) as tightly packed RGBA8. Without SDL_image, 8-bit
    PNGs still load (`j3.gfx.canvas.loadPng`). Empty when the file is missing or can't be read.
fn loadTexture(path: string): Texture
    Load an image as a texture; panics on failure.
fn tryLoadTextureC(path: cstr): Texture
    Try to load a texture from a C string path. Empty when the file is missing, or when
    SDL_image is missing and it isn't an 8-bit PNG.
fn tryLoadTexture(path: string): Texture
    Try to load a texture. Empty when the file is missing, or when SDL_image is missing and
    it isn't an 8-bit PNG.
fn textureFromCanvas(canvas: Canvas): Texture
    Upload a generated canvas once, with filtering selected by its raster mode.
    The canvas can be released after upload. Call after `init` or `initGl`.
fn updateTextureFromCanvas(tex: Texture, canvas: Canvas, x: i32, y: i32, w: i32, h: i32)
    Update a clipped rectangle of a texture from a canvas of the same size.
    Direct writes to `canvas.pixels` work: pass the changed rectangle explicitly.
fn drawTexture(tex: Texture, pos: Vec2)
    Draw a texture at its native size.
fn drawTextureAt(tex: Texture, dest: Vec2, destSize: Vec2)
    Draw a texture into a screen rect.
fn drawTextureSrc(tex: Texture, src: Vec2, srcSize: Vec2, dest: Vec2, destSize: Vec2)
    Draw a source sub-rect of a texture into a dest rect.
fn drawTextureCam(cam: Camera, tex: Texture, pos: Vec2)
    Draw a texture in world space through `cam`.
fn drawTextureAtCam(cam: Camera, tex: Texture, dest: Vec2, destSize: Vec2)
    Draw a sized texture in world space through `cam`.
fn drawTextureEx(tex: Texture, src: Vec2, srcSize: Vec2, dest: Vec2, destSize: Vec2, angle: f32, flip: int, tint: Color)
    Draw a source rect with rotation, flip, and tint. `flip` is 0, 1 (horizontal), or 2 (vertical).
fn drawTextureExCam(cam: Camera, tex: Texture, src: Vec2, srcSize: Vec2, dest: Vec2, destSize: Vec2, angle: f32, flip: int, tint: Color)
    `drawTextureEx` in world space through `cam`.
fn drawLine(a: Vec2, b: Vec2)
    Stroke a line in screen pixels using the current draw color.
fn drawTextureSrcCam(cam: Camera, tex: Texture, src: Vec2, srcSize: Vec2, dest: Vec2, destSize: Vec2)
    Draw a source sub-rect in world space through `cam`.
fn destroyTexture(tex: Texture)
    Free a texture.
fn simKey(key: Key, down: bool)
    Fake a key going down (`down`) or up, as if SDL sent it: on the next `poll`, `keyPressed` / `keyPressedRaw` (down)
    or `keyReleased` (up) is true for one frame, and `keyDown` holds in between. A down while it is already held is an
    auto-repeat (no new press). Wakes an idle-sleeping loop. Headless tests and agents: `--headless` runs see it like a
    real key. Shift/Ctrl/Alt/Super are the left keys. 64 queued at most per poll (more are dropped).
fn simText(s: string)
    Fake typed text, as if from `SDL_TEXTINPUT`: it shows in `textTyped` on the next `poll` (32 bytes per poll, the
    rest on the polls after), whether or not `textInput` is on. Ends a `simPreedit` composition. Up to 1024 bytes queued.
fn simPreedit(s: string, caret: int)
    Fake an IME composition, as if from `SDL_TEXTEDITING`: `textComposing` is `s` (31 bytes at most) and
    `textComposingCaret` is `caret` (a byte offset) from the next `poll` until another composition, `simText` or `""`.
fn simDrop(path: string)
    Fake a file dropped on the window, as if from `SDL_DROPFILE`: `droppedFile` is `path` (1023 bytes at most) on the next `poll`.
fn simMouse(x: f32, y: f32)
    Fake the cursor moving to (`x`, `y`) in window pixels, as SDL reports them: `mousePos` then reads it through the
    same `setVirtualSize` / DPI mapping as a real cursor. Once called, it replaces the real cursor for the rest of the run.
    `mouseDelta` does not move.
fn simButton(btn: Mouse, down: bool)
    Fake `btn` going down (`down`) or up at the `simMouse` position, as if SDL sent it: on the next `poll`
    `mousePressed` (down) or `mouseReleased` (up) is true for one frame, `mouseDown` holds in between. A down and an up
    in one frame still give each its own frame, like a real quick click.
fn simWheel(dy: f32)
    Fake wheel motion: `mouseWheel` is `dy` (whole notches, up is positive) on the next `poll`.
fn poll(): f32
    Snapshot input, drain events, and return dt in seconds.
fn requestQuit()
    Request that the main loop exit.
fn keyPressed(key: Key): bool
    True on the frame `key` went down. False while a UI text box has the keyboard (`keysTaken`).
fn keyReleased(key: Key): bool
    True on the frame `key` went up. False while a UI text box has the keyboard (`keysTaken`).
fn keyPressedRaw(key: Key): bool
    `keyPressed` even while a UI text box has the keyboard: for keys the game keeps (a screenshot key, quit).
fn takeKeys(on: bool)
    The UI owns the keyboard (`j3.ui.dom` sets this while a text box, select or grid has focus):
    `keyDown`, `keyPressed` and `keyReleased` read false until `takeKeys(false)` and the next `poll`.
    `keyDownRaw` / `keyPressedRaw` still see every key.
fn keysTaken(): bool
    Whether a UI text box has the keyboard (`takeKeys`).
fn mousePressed(btn: Mouse): bool
    True on the frame `btn` went down.
fn mouseReleased(btn: Mouse): bool
    True on the frame `btn` went up.
fn mouseWheel(): f32
    Mouse wheel delta accumulated this frame.
fn captureMouse(on: bool)
    Enable or disable relative mouse capture.
fn mouseCaptured(): bool
    Whether relative mouse capture is on.
fn mouseDelta(): Vec2
    Relative mouse motion since last `poll`.
fn padPressed(i: int, btn: Pad): bool
    True on the frame pad `i` pressed `btn`.
fn textInput(on: bool)
    Start or stop SDL text input. Characters land in `textTyped` on the next `poll`.
fn textComposingCaret(): int
    Byte offset of the IME caret inside `textComposing` (0 when there is no composition).
fn textTyped(): string
    UTF-8 text entered since the previous `poll`. Empty when text input is off.
fn textComposing(): string
    The IME composition string being typed (not yet committed), or empty. Committed text arrives in `textTyped`.
fn textInputRect(x: f32, y: f32, w: f32, h: f32)
    Tell the IME where the caret is, in the 2D drawing pixels of `mousePos` (its candidate list opens
    beside it); mapped through the letterbox, `setUiScale` and the high-DPI scale to window units.
fn droppedFile(): string
    Path of a file dropped on the window this frame, or empty.
fn clipboard(): string
    Text on the system clipboard, or empty. A view of a buffer that the next call replaces.
fn primarySelection(): string
    Text of the PRIMARY selection (X11/Wayland: the last text selected anywhere, pasted with a middle
    click), or empty. Empty when SDL is older than 2.26 or the platform has none. A view of the buffer
    `clipboard()` also uses; the next call of either replaces it.
fn setPrimarySelection(s: string)
    Put `s` on the PRIMARY selection (call it when a selection ends, the way Linux terminals copy on
    select). Does nothing when SDL is older than 2.26 or the platform has none.
enum Cursor
    System mouse cursor shapes (SDL's numbering).
    Arrow
    Text
    Crosshair
    ResizeAcross
    ResizeUpDown
    Move
    Hand
    Wait
    NotAllowed

fn setCursor(c: Cursor)
    Show cursor `c` this frame; with no call the arrow returns.
fn setClipboard(s: string)
    Put `s` on the system clipboard (any length; text after a NUL byte is cut).
fn keyDown(key: Key): bool
    True while `key` is held. False while a UI text box has the keyboard (`keysTaken`).
fn keyDownRaw(key: Key): bool
    `keyDown` even while a UI text box has the keyboard.
fn mousePos(): Vec2
    Cursor position in window pixels, or in virtual pixels under `setVirtualSize` (outside
    `0..w` / `0..h` over the bars). On a frame a button went down or up it is where that happened,
    not where the cursor is now, so a click made while moving hits what was under it.
fn mouseDown(btn: Mouse): bool
    True while `btn` is held.
fn padDown(i: int, btn: Pad): bool
    True while pad `i` holds `btn`.
fn padConnected(i: int): bool
    True while pad `i` (0 or 1) is plugged in and open.
fn padAxis(i: int, axis: Axis): f32
    Normalized pad axis with a small deadzone.
fn fontFallback(font: Font, extra: Font)
    Add `extra` to the fallback chain of `font` (see above). Duplicates and a full chain are ignored.
fn fontFallbackPath(font: Font, path: string)
    A fallback font by file: recorded now, opened at `font`'s size on the first code point `font` and the fonts
    already in its chain lack, then appended like `fontFallback`. A missing file is never used. `destroyFont(font)`
    closes it. Needs `font` from `loadFont`/`tryLoadFont` (its size is known). At most 16 pending at once.
fn windowPixels(): Vec2
    Width and height `readWindow` reads, in window pixels (the whole window, bars included, whatever the
    virtual size). `out` needs `w * h * 4` bytes.
fn readWindow(out: []u8): bool
    Copy the frame drawn so far (before `present`) into `out` as RGBA8, top row first:
    window pixels wide and tall (the bars too under `setVirtualSize`), 4 bytes a pixel. False when it could not be read.
fn env(name: string): string
    `$name`, or "" when unset.
fn captureFrame(path: string): bool
    Save the frame drawn so far to `path` as a PNG. Call it after drawing and before
    `present`. False when the pixels could not be read or the file not written.
fn fixedDt(): f32
    `J3_FIXED_DT` as seconds (digits and one dot), or 0 when unset.
fn present()
    Show the frame. F3 toggles the profiler overlay, F4 the UI cost breakdown (`J3_F4=1` starts it open). `j3c run --frames N --screenshot FILE`
    sets `J3_FRAMES` and `J3_SCREENSHOT`: frame N is saved to FILE before it is shown, then
    quit is requested, and a `J3STATS` line of the run's averages is printed.
const IDLE_FOREVER: int = 1000000
    `idleReport(ms)` for "nothing is due": sleep until an event (the loop's cap still wakes it).

fn wakeAt(ms: int)
    Wake the idle loop by `ms` (a `ticks()` time), e.g. the next cursor blink edge or an app-logic timer ("bot plays at
    `ticks() >= due`"); canvas animation and `j3.thread.Queue` results wake it by themselves. The earliest pending call wins; it
    covers the next sleep only, so call it again each frame you still need it. While set it also replaces the N ms
    safety tick for that sleep. No effect when the loop is not sleeping.
fn idleClampWake(sleepMs: int, wakeAtMs: int, nowMs: int): int
    `sleepMs` shortened to end at `wakeAtMs` (0: none) seen at `nowMs`; 0 when that time has come. Pure.
fn idleReport(ms: int)
    Called by `Dom.endFrame`: this frame was a UI frame and the next is due in `ms` (0: now).
fn idleWake()
    Wake a sleeping loop from any thread (`Dom.requestFrame`, `j3.thread.Queue` pushes through `j3.wake`). A no-op without a
    window; wakes already queued are merged into one.
fn idleSleepMs(hosted: bool, allowed: bool, reportMs: int, capMs: int): int
    How long the loop may sleep, in ms (0: run a frame now). Pure, so a test can drive it with a fake state.
fn idleSkipAllowed(): bool
    Sleeping through idle frames is on: `J3_IDLE_SKIP=1` forces it, `=0` forbids it; otherwise on unless the run is a
    test or capture (`J3_FRAMES`, `J3_SCREENSHOT`, `J3_CAPTURE`, `J3_FIXED_DT`, `J3_STEADY`) or headless (`offscreen` / `dummy`
    video driver), where frame counts must hold.
fn framesPresented(): int
    Frames shown so far (`present` calls).
fn idleWait(): bool
    If the last UI frame said nothing is due, sleep in `SDL_WaitEventTimeout` until an event, the due time, or the cap, and
    return true; the caller then runs a frame. False: run a frame now. The waking event waits in `IDLE_EV` for `poll`.
fn idleWoke(): bool
    True once after a sleep: the UI clamps the first frame's dt to one frame (`Dom.beginFrame`).
