enum Mouse
    Mouse button.
    Left
        Left button.
    Right
        Right button.
    Middle
        Middle button.
    Back
        Side button "back" (SDL X1, X11 button 8).
    Forward
        Side button "forward" (SDL X2, X11 button 9).

enum Pad
    Gamepad face, shoulder, or d-pad button.
    A
        Face A / Cross.
    B
        Face B / Circle.
    X
        Face X / Square.
    Y
        Face Y / Triangle.
    Back
        Back / Select.
    Start
        Start.
    LB
        Left bumper.
    RB
        Right bumper.
    Up
        D-pad up.
    Down
        D-pad down.
    Left
        D-pad left.
    Right
        D-pad right.

enum Axis
    Gamepad stick or trigger axis.
    LeftX
        Left stick X.
    LeftY
        Left stick Y.
    RightX
        Right stick X.
    RightY
        Right stick Y.
    LT
        Left trigger.
    RT
        Right trigger.

const RAMP_W: int = 256
    Gradient ramp atlas size: one baked color ramp per row.

const RAMP_ROWS: int = 64

fn p2Push(kind: int, x: f32, y: f32, w: f32, h: f32, u0: f32, v0: f32, du: f32, dv: f32, c1: Color, c2: Color, p0: f32, p1: f32, p2: f32, p3: f32, bw: f32, tex: u32)
    Queue one 2D instance in view-local logical pixels. `kind`: 0 flat color, 1 `tex` sampled
    through `u0 v0 du dv` and tinted by `c1`, 2 rounded rect (`p0..p3` radii tl tr br bl,
    `bw` border width, `c2` border color), 3 circle (`p0` radius), 4 ring (`p0` outer radius,
    `bw` width), 5 line (`p0 p1` to `p2 p3`, `bw` radius; the rect is its bounds), 6 gradient
    rounded rect (kind 2, plus `u0 v0 du dv` = angle, ramp row, type 0 linear 1 radial 2 conic,
    flag 1 for the gradient on the border), 7 outer box shadow (`u0 v0` half size of the box, `du`
    sigma; the rect is the grown quad), 8 inset shadow (`u0 v0` offset, `du` sigma, `dv` spread),
    9 dashed or dotted border (`u0` 1 dashed 2 dotted, `v0` period). Shapes are antialiased in the
    shader. Kind 10 is a rounded framebuffer texture sampled in physical screen coordinates. Kind 12 is a
    filled outline queued by `p2Poly`. A line's quad runs along it in the shader; the rect only culls.
    Batches until the texture or clip changes; `p2Flush` draws them.
    `j3.gfx.paint` is the public face of this.
fn p2Watch(on: bool)
    Start (`true`) or stop folding every queued instance into a running hash (`p2Watched`). `Dom` watches `<canvas>` bodies, so
    an idle-sleeping app keeps drawing while a canvas's output changes (hand-drawn animation) and sleeps once it holds still.
fn p2Watched(): u64
    The hash of the instances queued while watched since the last call, and reset it (0: none).
fn p2RampRow(row: int, texels: ptr)
    Write one baked color ramp (`RAMP_W` RGBA8 texels, straight alpha) into ramp atlas row `row`.
    Queued draws go out first, since the row may be one they still sample.
fn p2Flush()
    Draw everything queued and restore the GL state `j3.gfx` expects (depth test and culling on,
    blending and scissor off). Cheap when nothing is queued. Call it before raw GL; `j3.gfx`,
    `present`, `readWindow`, and texture deletion or update already do.
fn p2BackdropBase(): u32
    Complete destination behind the current isolated group, supplied by the post pipeline.
fn p2SetBackdropBase(tex: u32)
fn p2SetBackdropLazy(hook: fn())
fn p2BackdropLazy(): bool
fn p2BackdropBlur(x: f32, y: f32, w: f32, h: f32, tl: f32, tr: f32, br: f32, bl: f32, radius: f32, alpha: f32)
    Blur the current framebuffer behind one rounded paint primitive. `radius` is sigma in
    view-local px (paint clamps its public input to 64). Requires a single-sample color buffer
    at window size. Two full-window RGBA8 targets are reused, resized, and released with GL.
    Existing batches go out first; subsequent paint retains its clip, mask, view and affine map.
fn p2Clip(on: bool, x0: f32, y0: f32, x1: f32, y1: f32)
    Clip later 2D draws to a rect in view-local logical pixels (axis-aligned; `on = false`
    removes it). Queued draws go out first. `j3.gfx.paint.pushClip` manages the nesting.
fn p2Shape(slot: int, pts: ptr, n: int, evenOdd: bool)
    Clip later 2D draws to an outline (slot 0 or 1; `n = 0` removes it): `pts` is `n` x, y float pairs in
    view-local logical pixels (the space of `p2Push`, before `p2Affine`), at most `path.MAXPT` points; a point with
    x below -1e8 separates contours. Coverage is antialiased in the shader; `evenOdd` picks the fill rule.
    The caller keeps `pts` alive while the shape is set. Queued draws go out first.
    `j3.gfx.paint.pushShape` manages the nesting.
fn p2Poly(pts: []f32, n: int, evenOdd: bool, s: f32, tx: f32, ty: f32, c: Color)
    Queue a filled outline (`j3.gfx.paint.fillShape`): `n` x, y pairs of `pts` mapped by `p * s + (tx, ty)` into
    view-local logical pixels, a point with x below -1e8 starting a contour, each contour closed, `evenOdd` or
    nonzero. The points go into the batch's rows of the shape texture, so outlines batch like any other shape
    (no flush per path); a full set of rows flushes. At most `MAXPT` points.
fn p2ClipShapes(n: int, pts: ptr, c0: int, c1: int, c2: int, c3: int)
    Clip later 2D draws to the intersection of up to four convex outlines (`n` of them, 0 removes): `pts` holds
    `n` rows of 128 floats (x, y pairs, at most 64 points each, closed), `c0..c3` the point counts, all in the
    frame `p2Affine` turns (like `p2Shape`). Coverage is antialiased in the shader. The caller keeps `pts` alive.
    Queued draws go out first. `j3.gfx.paint.pushClip` manages these under rotation and skew.
fn p2Mask(kind: int, row: int, angle: f32, x: f32, y: f32, w: f32, h: f32)
    Multiply the alpha of later 2D draws by a gradient ramp row (see `paint.gradient`) laid over the box
    `x y w h` (view-local logical pixels): `kind` 1 linear at `angle` radians (CSS: 0 is up), 2 radial
    from the center; 0 removes it. Queued draws go out first. `j3.gfx.paint.pushMask` manages the nesting.
fn p2Affine(on: bool, a: f32, b: f32, c: f32, d: f32, tx: f32, ty: f32)
    Turn later draws by an affine map of view-local logical pixels (`x' = a x + c y + tx`,
    `y' = b x + d y + ty`; `on = false` removes it). Shapes are drawn in their own frame and only
    their corners move, so edges stay antialiased; the clip rect and scissor are not rotated.
    Queued draws go out first. `j3.gfx.paint.pushRotate` manages the nesting.
fn p2PushView(pos: Vec2, size: Vec2)
    Draw later 2D into a sub-rectangle of the window: `(0, 0)` becomes `pos` (relative to the
    current view) and the width and height are `size`, in logical pixels. Nests; flushes.
fn p2PopView()
    Undo the last `p2PushView`. Flushes.
fn p2SurfaceBegin(w: int, h: int)
fn p2SurfaceEnd()
fn p2ScreenPoint(p: Vec2): Vec2
    Map a view-local paint point through its affine and viewport into physical top-left pixels.
fn p2CompositeTexture(tex: u32, alpha: f32)
    Composite a premultiplied full-surface texture in framebuffer coordinates, applying the
    current clip/mask once. The inverse affine expands its quad without transforming the pixels.
fn p2CompositeRect(tex: u32, alpha: f32, x: f32, y: f32, w: f32, h: f32)
    `p2CompositeTexture` for a texture that holds only the physical rect (x, y, w, h; top-left, framebuffer coords
    relative to `fbRect`) of the surface: the quad covers just that rect. `j3.gfx.post` bounded effect groups.
fn p2TextureFilter(tex: u32, nearest: bool)
    Switch a texture between nearest and linear sampling (`image-rendering: pixelated`). Draws queued with the
    old filter go out first. No-op without GL.
fn bakeTextBold(font: Font, msg: string): Texture
    Like `bakeText`, emboldened. The caller owns the texture.
fn drawTextBytes(font: Font, pos: Vec2, msg: []u8)
    Draw UTF-8 bytes. ASCII comes from the glyph atlas. Other strings reuse a cached raster.
fn p2Text(font: Font, pos: Vec2, msg: string, color: Color, scale: f32)
    Draw `msg` in `color`, glyphs scaled by `scale` (GL only; the SDL path ignores `scale`).
    `j3.gfx.paint.text` calls this.
fn p2TextGradient(font: Font, pos: Vec2, msg: string, color: Color, scale: f32, bold: bool, row: int, from: Vec2, to: Vec2)
    `p2Text` filled from ramp `row` (see `j3.gfx.paint.gradient`) along the line `from` to `to`
    (already in view-local pixels); `color` supplies the alpha. Text that is not in the glyph atlas
    takes the gradient the same way. `j3.gfx.paint.textGradient` calls this.
fn p2TextBold(font: Font, pos: Vec2, msg: string, color: Color, scale: f32)
    `p2Text` with the glyphs emboldened (`TTF_STYLE_BOLD`), for `j3.gfx.paint.textBold`.
fn destroyFont(font: Font)
    Close a font and drop its cached rasters and glyph atlas.
fn textVisualRuns(font: Font, msg: []u8, out: []int): int
    The runs of `msg` in visual order as (byte start, byte end, level) triples into `out`; returns the run
    count, 0 when `msg` has no right-to-left run (then it is drawn in logical order). For tests and tools.
const ZONE_SHADOW: int = 0
    GPU time spent in the shadow pass.

const ZONE_CAMERA: int = 1
    GPU time spent in camera passes.

const ZONE_UI: int = 2
    GPU time spent drawing the 2D HUD over GL.

const ZONE_UI_BLUR: int = 3
    GPU time of `backdrop-filter` blurs (`p2BackdropBlur`).

const ZONE_UI_LAYER: int = 4
    GPU time of paint layers: `filter`, `mix-blend-mode`, 3D `perspective` passes and their composites (`j3.gfx.post`).

struct FrameStats
    One frame's numbers, or averages of them. Times are milliseconds.
    draws: int = 0
        Draw calls through `j3.gfx`; an instanced draw counts once.
    tris: int = 0
        Triangles those draws submitted.
    frameMs: f32 = 0
        Present to present.
    cpuMs: f32 = 0
        CPU time from the last swap to this present (excludes waiting on vsync).
    sceneMs: f32 = 0
        CPU time inside `scene3d.draw`.
    shadowMs: f32 = 0
        GPU time of the shadow pass; -1 when no fresh GPU sample is available.
    cameraMs: f32 = 0
        GPU time of the camera passes; -1 when no fresh GPU sample is available.
    uiMs: f32 = 0
        GPU time of the HUD; -1 when no fresh GPU sample is available.

fn lastFrameStats(): FrameStats
    Most recently completed frame, without smoothing. GPU timings arrive asynchronously.
fn frameStats(): FrameStats
    Averages over the last second or so of frames.
fn profDraw(tris: int)
    Count one draw call of `tris` triangles. `j3.gfx` calls this.
fn drawsNow(): int
    Draw calls counted so far in the frame being built (tests compare it before and after a paint).
fn profScene(us: int)
    Add `us` microseconds of CPU time to `sceneMs`. `j3.scene3d.draw` calls this.
fn profZone(z: int)
    Time GPU work as zone `z` (`ZONE_*`) until the next zone or `present`; -1 stops timing.
    A zone started twice in a frame (two camera views) adds up.
const UI_BUILD: int = 0
    `UI_NOW` / `UI_AVG` slots. CPU ms of the j3ui phases: build (`beginFrame` to `layout`: input, the app's update,
    the markup walk), style (`resolveStyles`), layout, paint (`paintAll`, `inputs.overlay`), and the part of paint spent
    in effect passes (`UI_FX`).

const UI_STYLE: int = 1

const UI_LAYOUT: int = 2

const UI_PAINT: int = 3

const PROF_BLURS: int = 9

const PROF_LAYERS: int = 10

fn profUiStart()
    Start timing one j3ui root's frame (`Dom.beginFrame`).
fn profUiMark(k: int)
    Add the CPU time since the last mark to phase `k` (`UI_BUILD` .. `UI_PAINT`).
fn profUiCount(k: int)
    Add one to count `k` (`PROF_BLURS`, `PROF_LAYERS`).
fn profUiZone(z: int): int
    Time the GPU work that follows as zone `z` (`ZONE_UI_BLUR`, `ZONE_UI_LAYER`; 0 is plain paint) and return the zone
    it replaces, for the caller to put back. Queued paint goes out first, under the old zone. The CPU time spent while
    any effect zone is open is `UI_FX`.
fn tryLoadFontC(path: cstr, size: int): Font
    Try to load a font from a C string path. Empty when the file or SDL_ttf is missing.
fn tryLoadFont(path: string, size: int): Font
    Try to load a font. Empty when the file or SDL_ttf is missing.
fn loadFont(path: string, size: int): Font
    Load a font at point size; panics on failure.
fn textShaping(): bool
    True when the installed SDL_ttf shapes text with HarfBuzz (2.20+ built with it): Arabic letters join,
    Indic and other complex scripts reorder, ligatures form. Without it text is laid out glyph by glyph.
fn fontShape(font: Font, rtl: bool, script: string)
    Direction and script SDL_ttf shapes `font` with from now on (`rtl` true = right to left; `script` is an
    ISO 15924 tag such as "Arab", "" keeps the last). Only meaningful with `textShaping()`; a no-op otherwise.
fn bakeTextBytes(font: Font, msg: []u8): Texture
    Rasterize UTF-8 bytes to a texture using the current draw color.
fn bakeText(font: Font, msg: string): Texture
    Rasterize UTF-8 text to a texture.
fn textSize(font: Font, msg: string): Vec2
    Pixel width and height of `msg`. Zero when `font` is empty or measurement fails.
    ASCII reuses the glyph atlas. Other strings reuse a cached measurement.
fn textSizeBytes(font: Font, msg: []u8): Vec2
    Pixel width and height of UTF-8 bytes. Zero when `font` is empty or measurement fails.
    ASCII reuses the glyph atlas. Other strings reuse a cached measurement.
fn drawText(font: Font, pos: Vec2, msg: string)
    Draw `msg`. ASCII comes from the glyph atlas. Other strings reuse a cached raster.
fn drawTextBold(font: Font, pos: Vec2, msg: string)
    Like `drawText`, but SDL_ttf emboldens the glyphs.
fn textSizeBold(font: Font, msg: string): Vec2
    Like `textSize`, measured with the bold face.
struct TextMode
    Typographic options for the measure and draw calls (`textWidth`, `p2Text`, the caret and hit-test helpers). The default
    (all off) is what app code gets; the `.j3ui` runtime sets it from the style of the element it is measuring or painting
    (`j3.ui.dom.enterText`) and puts the previous value back after. Each field is read in one place: `textWidthPlain`
    (measure), `atlasDraw` (glyph pen) and `p2TextRun` (runs cut at digits or code points).
    tabular: bool
        `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`: every ASCII digit has the widest digit's advance, centred in it.
    letterSp: f32
        `letter-spacing`: extra px after every glyph (code point), the last of a run included, as a browser adds it. Ignored in shaped
        (complex script, right to left) runs, which a browser does not space either.
    wordSp: f32
        `word-spacing`: extra px after every U+0020 (on top of `letterSp`).
    shaped: bool
        ASCII is rastered whole like other text instead of drawn from the glyph atlas, so SDL_ttf's shaping applies
        (ligatures such as `->` in Fira Code, when `textShaping()`). Costs a cached raster per distinct string.

fn setTextMode(m: TextMode): TextMode
    Make `m` the text mode and return the one it replaced, so a caller can restore it. Not thread safe, like all of `j3.game` text.
fn textWidth(font: Font, msg: []u8, bold: bool): f32
    Width of UTF-8 `msg` for layout: the sum of per-glyph advances (the same widths the GL glyph
    atlas draws with), and `TTF_SizeUTF8` on each run of non-ASCII bytes. With a fallback chain
    (`fontFallback`) or a complex script (`textShaping`) each run is measured with its own font and
    shaping, exactly as `p2Text` draws it. Needs SDL_ttf, no window or GL
    (unlike `textSize`, whose ASCII path builds the atlas). Zero for an empty font.
fn textBidi(font: Font, msg: []u8): bool
    Whether `msg` is drawn reordered (it holds a right-to-left run), so caret and selection need `textCaretX` and friends.
fn textCaretX(font: Font, msg: []u8, bold: bool, i: int): f32
    Visual x of the caret before byte `i` of `msg` as `p2Text` draws it. Inside a right-to-left run x decreases
    as `i` grows. At a boundary between runs the caret belongs to the run of the preceding character
    (index 0: the first run in logical order). Plain text: `textWidth(msg[0..i])`.
fn textIndexAt(font: Font, msg: []u8, bold: bool, x: f32): int
    The caret byte index nearest to visual x `x` (0 is the line's left edge) in reordered `msg`; -1 when `msg` has no
    right-to-left run (use the logical hit test).
fn textSelRects(font: Font, msg: []u8, bold: bool, lo: int, hi: int): int
    The visual rectangles (x, width; read with `selRectX` / `selRectW`) covering bytes `lo..hi` of `msg`: one per
    intersected run in visual order, adjacent ones merged. Returns how many. Plain text gives one.
fn selRectX(k: int): f32
fn selRectW(k: int): f32
fn fontHas(font: Font, cp: int): bool
    Whether `font` or a font of its fallback chain has a glyph for code point `cp` (true when it cannot tell: an empty font, no SDL_ttf query).
fn fontLine(font: Font): int
    `TTF_FontHeight`: the natural line height of `font` (0 for an empty font).
fn fontAscent(font: Font): int
    `TTF_FontAscent`: baseline distance from the top of a line (0 for an empty font).
struct Finger
    One finger on the touch screen, as `game.finger(i)` reports it.
    id: int
        SDL's finger id; stable from down to up, reused later.
    x: f32
        Position in the pixels of `mousePos` (virtual pixels under `setVirtualSize`).
    y: f32
        See `x`.
    down: bool
        True for exactly one frame, the one the finger touched down on.
    held: bool
        True while the finger is on the screen (including the down frame, not the up frame).
    up: bool
        True for exactly one frame, the one the finger lifted on; it is gone after that.

fn fingerCount(): int
    Fingers on the screen this frame (up to 10), including ones that lifted this frame.
fn finger(i: int): Finger
    The `i`th finger, `0 <= i < fingerCount()`, oldest slot first. Out of range gives `id` -1 and nothing set.
fn backgrounded(): bool
    True between the OS sending the app to the background and bringing it back. There is no GL surface then:
    `App.run` skips update, draw and swap, and a hand-written loop should too (call `waitBackground` instead).
fn waitBackground()
    Sleep until an event arrives or N ms pass, for a loop that is backgrounded. Resets the frame clock so
    the first frame after resume does not see the time spent away.
fn backKeyAnywhere(on: bool)
    Treat the Back scancode as a close request on every platform. Only Android does by default, since some
    desktop keyboards and mice send it; this exists so tests can drive the Android path.
fn init(title: string, width: int, height: int)
    Create a centered accelerated 2D window and renderer.
fn initGl(title: string, width: int, height: int)
    Create a centered OpenGL 3.3 core window and context. With `J3_HIDDEN` set the window never shows (what
    `j3c run --headless` does on Windows, whose SDL offscreen driver has no GL).
fn initGlHidden(title: string, width: int, height: int)
    An OpenGL 3.3 core context whose window never shows, for rendering to files (renders,
    screenshots). No audio or game pads. `SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen` runs it without a display.
struct ContextHints
    What a GL window asks the driver for (`setContextHints`). `ContextHints {}` is the default: 24 depth bits,
    no stencil, no MSAA, no debug context. A 2D-only app can pass `depth: 0` to skip the depth buffer; then
    anything that needs depth (`j3.scene3d`, meshes, `gfx.depthBias`) draws without depth testing.
    depth: int = 24
    stencil: int = 0
    samples: int = 0
        MSAA samples of the window framebuffer (0 = none).
    debug: bool = false

struct ContextInfo
    What the driver granted (`contextInfo`): bit counts, MSAA samples, debug flag, and the GL version as
    `major * 100 + minor` (`es` for OpenGL ES). All zero before a GL window exists.
    depth: int
    stencil: int
    samples: int
    debug: bool
    version: int
    es: bool

fn setContextHints(h: ContextHints)
    Set the GL context attributes before the window is created (`App.create`, `initGl`); ignored after.
fn contextInfo(): ContextInfo
    The attributes the driver actually granted (see `ContextInfo`).
fn isGles(): bool
    Whether the context is OpenGL ES (Android, or `J3_GLES=1` on desktop). Set once when the
    context is made, from the profile SDL reports; false before `initGl` and headless.
fn prepGl(src: string): string
    `src` ready for `glShaderSource` on the current context: unchanged on desktop, rewritten for
    GLSL ES 3.00 on ES (`j3.gfx.glsl.prep`). Every shader compile goes through it; call it at
    compile time and hand the result to GL at once.
fn floatTargets(): bool
    Whether `RGBA16F` can be a framebuffer attachment: always on desktop, on ES only with
    `GL_EXT_color_buffer_float` or `GL_EXT_color_buffer_half_float`. Checked once; when missing it
    says so once (stderr, and SDL_Log on Android) and callers use an `RGBA8` target instead.
fn setHiDpi(on: bool)
    Ask for a high-DPI drawable (`SDL_WINDOW_ALLOW_HIGHDPI`) when `initGl` creates the window, and
    lay out in logical pixels: `windowSize`, `mousePos` and 2D drawing then work in window units and
    are scaled by `dpiScale` onto the larger drawable (text stays sharp). Call it before `initGl`.
    On Android the scale is the screen density (densityDpi / 160). Off by default, so 3D games
    keep drawing in real pixels. `J3_UI_SCALE=1.5` forces a scale
    without it (GL only; a letterboxed `setVirtualSize` already fits the physical window).
fn dpiScale(): f32
    Physical pixels per logical pixel for 2D drawing and `windowSize` (1 without `setHiDpi` or
    `J3_UI_SCALE`). Fractional on Linux scaling setups.
fn usingGl(): bool
    Whether this session uses an OpenGL context.
fn glProc(name: string): ptr
    Resolve an OpenGL entry point by name.
fn setGlShutdown(cb: fn())
    Register a callback run before the GL context is deleted.
fn addGlShutdown(cb: fn())
    Register another callback without replacing an existing GL renderer's teardown.
fn destroy()
    Release controllers, GL/renderer, window, and subsystems.
fn holdClose(on: bool)
    Let the game decide what closing the window does: it then only raises `closeRequested`.
fn closeRequested(): bool
    True on the frame the window close button was hit while `holdClose` is on.
fn quitRequested(): bool
    Whether quit was requested.
fn setResizable(on: bool)
    Allow the user to resize the window.
fn setMinSize(w: int, h: int)
    Set the window minimum size.
fn setWindowIcon(icon: Canvas)
    Set the window's title-bar and taskbar icon from an RGBA canvas (`j3.gfx.canvas`; 64x64 is plenty).
    `App.run` sets one by itself: `[dist] icon` when it is a PNG, else the J3 logo.
fn setWindowTitle(title: string)
    Set the window title (copied; up to the `cstr` scratch size). No window yet: no effect.
fn setMaxSize(w: int, h: int)
    Set the window maximum size.
fn setWindowSize(w: int, h: int)
    Resize the window and cached size.
enum WindowHit
    What a point of an undecorated window does when pressed (`setWindowHitTest`). `Drag` moves the
    window, the `Resize*` edges resize it, `Normal` is an ordinary click for the app.
    Normal
    Drag
    ResizeTop
    ResizeTopRight
    ResizeRight
    ResizeBottomRight
    ResizeBottom
    ResizeBottomLeft
    ResizeLeft
    ResizeTopLeft

struct WinRect
    A rectangle in UI coordinates (what `mousePos` and a `.j3ui` layout use) for `setDragRegions`.
    x: f32
    y: f32
    w: f32
    h: f32

fn setDecorated(on: bool)
    Show (`true`, the default) or hide the system title bar and border. Undecorated, the window stays
    resizable, movable and maximizable: an invisible band of `setResizeBorder` px along its edges resizes
    it, and `setDragRegions` (or `window-drag` in `.j3ui`) says what moves it. Before the window exists
    it only records, so it opens that way (`App.undecorated`). Windowed mode only; headless a no-op.
    Linux: X11 and Wayland (SDL's Wayland driver, e.g. `SDL_VIDEODRIVER=wayland`; SDL2 picks X11/XWayland
    on GNOME by default). On Wayland the compositor must allow client-side decorations (GNOME does).
fn setTransparent(on: bool)
    Ask for a window whose pixels' alpha shows the desktop through (a compositor is needed; without one it looks
    opaque). Call before the GL window opens (`App.transparent`); OpenGL windows only. X11: an ARGB (depth 32)
    visual (`SDL_VIDEO_X11_WINDOW_VISUALID`); Wayland/EGL: an RGBA8 config with transparency allowed. The frame is
    premultiplied alpha, which normal blending over a cleared buffer already produces; see `setBackgroundAlpha`.
fn transparent(): bool
    Whether the window was asked to be see-through (`setTransparent`).
fn setBackgroundAlpha(a: f32)
    How opaque the background is in a transparent window, 0..1 (default 1): `clearTo` multiplies its alpha by it
    (premultiplied, so the desktop shows through at `1 - a`), and backgrounds that ask (`j3.term.view` default
    background) draw with it. Text and everything else drawn opaque stay opaque. No effect on an opaque window.
fn backgroundAlpha(): f32
    The background alpha in effect: `setBackgroundAlpha` in a transparent window, else 1.
fn setWindowClass(name: string)
    The window class the desktop groups the window by: X11 `WM_CLASS` (instance and class both `name`) and the
    Wayland `app_id`, which a `.desktop` file's `StartupWMClass` matches (a terminal's `--class`). Call before the
    window opens (`App.windowClass`); later calls change nothing. Wins over the `SDL_VIDEO_*_WMCLASS` the `.deb`
    launcher exports.
fn minimizeWindow()
    Minimize (iconify) the window.
fn showWindow()
    Show a window hidden with `hideWindow` (it comes back to the taskbar). For a drop-down "quick
    terminal" on a global hotkey (`j3.game.hotkey`):
    if hk.pressed() {
    if game.windowShown() && game.windowFocused() { game.hideWindow(); }
    else { game.showWindow(); game.raiseWindow(); }
    }
fn hideWindow()
    Hide the window: it leaves the screen and the taskbar; the loop keeps running.
fn raiseWindow()
    Bring the window to the front with keyboard focus, restoring it first when minimized. The window
    manager may still refuse focus (focus-stealing prevention); X11 window managers usually allow it.
fn windowShown(): bool
    False after `hideWindow` (and headless before the window exists); true when shown, also minimized.
fn windowMaximized(): bool
    Whether the window is maximized now, also after a maximize by the window manager (shortcut, snap,
    double-click). For a maximize/restore button: `setMaximized(!windowMaximized())`.
fn closeWindow()
    Close the window the way the window manager's close button does: quit (`App` shutdown callbacks
    run), or only `closeRequested` under `holdClose`. Takes effect at the next `poll`.
fn setResizeBorder(px: int)
    Width in points of the invisible resize band along the edges of an undecorated window (default 6;
    0 turns it off). Not active while maximized, fullscreen or not resizable.
fn setDragRegions(rects: []WinRect)
    Areas (UI coordinates) that move the window when dragged. Replaces the last set; set it every frame
    from layout. Double-clicking one toggles maximize. At most 32 (more are ignored).
fn setNoDragRegions(rects: []WinRect)
    Areas inside the drag regions that stay ordinary clicks (the title bar's buttons). Set every frame.
fn setWindowHitTest(f: fn(int, int): WindowHit)
    Decide yourself what a press does: `f(x, y)` gets the point in UI coordinates and replaces the drag
    regions (the resize band still comes first). SDL calls it from inside the event pump (`poll`) on the
    main thread, on X11 and Wayland only for a left-button press: it may read any game state, but must
    not call SDL window functions (no `setMaximized` there). On Windows it runs on every mouse move.
enum WindowMode
    How the game window sits on the screen.
    Windowed
        A normal window with a border, at the windowed size.
    Borderless
        Desktop-sized window with no border (`SDL_WINDOW_FULLSCREEN_DESKTOP`): fast alt-tab, desktop resolution.
    Fullscreen
        Exclusive fullscreen at the chosen resolution (`setResolution`).

fn setWindowMode(m: WindowMode)
    Switch between windowed, borderless and exclusive fullscreen. Called before `init` it only
    records the mode, and the window opens that way. Leaving fullscreen restores the windowed size.
fn windowMode(): WindowMode
    Current mode.
fn setResolution(w: int, h: int)
    Set the resolution. Windowed: resizes and recenters. Exclusive fullscreen: switches the display
    to the closest mode. Borderless always uses the desktop size, so this only records it.
fn displayCount(): int
    Number of connected monitors.
fn currentDisplay(): int
    Monitor the window is on.
fn displayName(i: int): string
    Monitor name from the system ("DELL U2723QE"), or "Display N" if it has none.
fn moveToDisplay(i: int)
    Move the window to monitor `i`, centered, and keep the current mode there.
fn desktopSize(display: int): Vec2
    Desktop resolution of monitor `display`.
fn resolutionCount(display: int): int
    How many distinct resolutions monitor `display` offers (one per w x h, refresh rates folded).
fn resolutionAt(display: int, i: int): Vec2
    The `i`th resolution of monitor `display`, largest first. (0, 0) if out of range.
fn refreshRate(): int
    Refresh rate of the window's monitor in Hz (60 if unknown), for "cap at the monitor rate".
fn setVsync(on: bool)
    Wait for the display refresh when presenting. GL changes it live; the 2D renderer fixes it at
    `init`, so call this before `init` there.
fn vsync(): bool
    Whether vsync is requested.
fn setFpsLimit(fps: int)
    Cap the frame rate by sleeping at `present` (0 = no cap). Not applied under `--frames`.
fn fpsLimit(): int
    Current cap, 0 for none.
fn windowFocused(): bool
    False while another window has focus, to pause or mute a game.
fn setMaximized(on: bool)
    Maximize or restore a windowed window.
fn windowPos(): Vec2
    Window top-left in desktop coordinates; (0, 0) before the window exists. `j3.app` saves it for hot restarts.
fn setWindowPos(x: int, y: int)
    Move the window's top-left to desktop coordinates (x, y).
fn centerWindow()
    Center the window on its monitor.
fn windowSize(): Vec2
    Size to lay out and draw in: the virtual size once `setVirtualSize` is on (the same at any
    window size), else the window size in pixels.
fn drawSize(): Vec2
    Size the 3D side works in: the virtual size, else the drawable in physical pixels (`windowSize`
    divided by `dpiScale` is the logical size). Equal to `windowSize` unless `setHiDpi` or `J3_UI_SCALE` is on.
fn setVirtualSize(w: int, h: int)
    Draw at a fixed `w` by `h` size whatever the window is, letterboxed: everything (2D, 3D,
    `.j3ui`) is laid out and drawn in these pixels, and `windowSize` returns them. The drawing
    area is the largest rect of that aspect ratio centered in the window, drawn at the real
    resolution (text and shapes stay sharp), and the bars around it are cleared to black each
    frame (`poll`). `mousePos` is in these pixels too: a pointer over a bar reads outside
    `0..w` / `0..h` (negative or past the size), not clamped. `mouseDelta` and `screenshot` /
    `readWindow` stay in window pixels. `setVirtualSize(0, 0)` turns it off (the default).
    Call it once at startup, or again to change; a window resize needs nothing.
    `screenRect` has the physical rect. `j3c doc testing` shows a letterboxed headless run.
fn virtualSize(): Vec2
    The size set by `setVirtualSize`, or 0 by 0 while it is off.
fn screenRect(): Rect
    Where the virtual screen is in the real window, in physical pixels (top-left origin): `x`, `y`
    offset, `w`, `h` size, and `s` window pixels per virtual pixel. The whole window with `s` 1
    while no virtual size is set. Use it only for code that needs real pixels (a `glViewport`,
    a readback); `windowSize` and `mousePos` are in virtual pixels.
fn p2LayerBegin()
fn p2LayerEnd()
fn p2LayerBounds(out: []f32): bool
    The open layer's drawn bounds into `out` (x0, y0, x1, y1 physical px, top-left, layer coords). False when the
    layer must be treated as full (raw GL drew into it). x1 <= x0 means nothing was drawn.
fn p2LayerMarkFull()
    Something drew into the open layer outside `p2Push` (3D meshes, raw GL): its effects run full-size.
fn p2FbRect(): GlRect
    `fbRect` as a GL bottom-left rect: what a copy from, or a viewport on, the current framebuffer takes.
fn p2GlRect(x: f32, y: f32, w: f32, h: f32): GlRect
    A virtual-pixel rect as a GL rect on the current framebuffer (rounded edges, no seams).
fn setUiScale(s: f32)
    Scale all screen-space 2D drawing (rects, textures, text) and `mousePos` by `s` (clamped 0.25..4).
    2 draws everything twice as big. Reset to 1 for overlays that should stay put.
fn uiScale(): f32
    Current 2D scale from `setUiScale`.
fn paintScale(): f32
    Physical pixels per 2D drawing unit: `setUiScale`, the letterbox scale and the high-DPI scale together.
    What a length in drawing units (a blur radius) is multiplied by to get framebuffer pixels.
fn uiSize(): Vec2
    Window size in scaled 2D units: what to lay a UI out in once `setUiScale` is set.
fn lastDt(): f32
    Dt from the last `poll`, in seconds.
fn setFrameAlpha(a: f32)
    Store the leftover fixed-step fraction. `j3.app` calls this before draw.
fn frameAlpha(): f32
    Leftover fixed-step fraction, 0..1. `0` is the previous tick, `1` is the latest.
fn frameCount(): int
    Frames since init.
fn ticks(): int
    Milliseconds since SDL started, for timing that shouldn't depend on frame rate.
fn delayMs(ms: int)
    Sleep the calling thread about `ms` milliseconds (`SDL_Delay`).
fn micros(): int
    Microseconds on a steady clock, for timing inside a frame.
